\subsection{Research attempt}
\paragraph{Model, date and investigated scope.}
OpenAI GPT-6 Astra (configured model \texttt{gpt-6-astra}), ultra reasoning; generated 11 October 2026. Verifies the formal metric constant-shift law and conditional compatibility with area scaling, while showing every fixed smooth regularization has local dimension two; does not identify the limiting LQG metric.

\paragraph{Sources and literature check.}
The complete supplied definition, including source qualifications and the dated literature assessment, was reviewed. The calculation below is an elementary restricted attempt motivated by [S2]; it does not claim novelty or a fresh literature-status determination.

\paragraph{Context and investigated scope.} The complete supplied definition, including source qualifications and the dated literature assessment, was reviewed. The calculation below is an elementary restricted attempt motivated by [S2]; it does not claim novelty or a fresh literature-status determination.

\paragraph{The constant-shift consistency test.}
Fix positive $\gamma$ and $\beta$, and first replace the generalized field by a smooth function $h_\varepsilon$. The proposed regularized length of a piecewise smooth path $\zeta$ is
\[
 L_{\varepsilon,h}(\zeta)=\int
 e^{\gamma h_\varepsilon(\zeta(t))/\beta}|\zeta'(t)|\,dt,
 \qquad D_{\varepsilon,h}(x,y)=\inf_{\zeta:x\to y}L_{\varepsilon,h}(\zeta).
\]
Assume the regularization preserves constants. For every constant $c\in\mathbb R$, every path length is multiplied by the same factor, so taking the infimum gives exactly
\[
 D_{\varepsilon,h+c}(x,y)=e^{\gamma c/\beta}D_{\varepsilon,h}(x,y).
\]
Any normalized distance limit using a normalization independent of this additive constant would inherit this identity, provided the limit exists.

A regularized Liouville area measure with field-independent renormalization and exponential factor $e^{\gamma h_\varepsilon}$ scales instead by $e^{\gamma c}$. If such a measure were identified with a covariantly normalized $\beta$-dimensional measure of the limiting metric, scaling all distances by $a$ would scale that measure by $a^\beta$. These two transformation laws are consistent precisely with $a=e^{\gamma c/\beta}$. This is a compatibility calculation under stated measure hypotheses, not a deduction of $\beta$ from the field or a proof that the area measure is the metric Hausdorff measure.

\paragraph{A regularization countercheck.}
For each fixed $\varepsilon>0$, on a compact coordinate region the smooth positive weight $e^{\gamma h_\varepsilon/\beta}$ has bounds $0<c_\varepsilon\leq w\leq C_\varepsilon<\infty$. Writing $D^{\rm patch}_{\varepsilon,h}$ for the distance with paths restricted to a sufficiently small convex coordinate patch, we have
\[
 c_\varepsilon|x-y|\leq D^{\rm patch}_{\varepsilon,h}(x,y)
 \leq C_\varepsilon|x-y|.
\]
The upper bound uses the straight path, and the lower bound uses Euclidean path length. Locally the smooth metric therefore has Hausdorff dimension two, whatever numerical value of $\beta$ appears in the exponential. This does not force the limiting dimension to be two: the comparison constants depend on $\varepsilon$ and need not remain bounded after normalization.

\paragraph{The unproved limiting operation.}
Neither calculation controls optimized paths as the field becomes rough. One still needs a specified normalization, tightness and nondegeneracy of the distance functions, identification of their limit with the intended LQG metric, and a proof of its dimension. In particular, the constant-shift identity cannot justify interchanging regularization with the infimum over paths. The source tensor remains a formal prescription; no pointwise exponential of a distribution or new formula for its Hausdorff dimension is asserted.

